<setup>

</setup>  
<p>
  We have seen that the degree <m>n</m> Taylor polynomial approximation of a function <m>f(x)</m> improves as <m>n</m> gets larger.  It is therefore natural to think about letting <m>n</m> increase without bound and to consider infinite sums that involve the variable <m>x</m>, as we did in the final activity of this section, where we saw that 
  <me> 
    \frac{1}{1-x} = 1 + x + x^2 + \cdots + x^n + \cdots
  </me>
  for all <m>x</m> such that <m>-1 \lt x \lt 1</m>.
</p>

<p>
  In the following questions, we consider several other infinite sums that involve a variable <m>x</m> and determine whether each sum is geometric.  To do so, we study the ratio between consecutive terms.  We will call the first term <m>a_1</m>, the second term <m>a_2</m>, and so on; in the example above with the sum <me>1 + x + x^2 + \cdots + x^n + \cdots</me> we have <m>a_1 = 1</m>, <m>a_2 = x</m>, and <m>a_3 = x^2</m>.
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        <m> \displaystyle S = 1 - \frac{x}{2} + \frac{x^2}{4} - \frac{x^3}{8} + \cdots + (-1)^n \frac{x^n}{2^n} + \cdots </m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m> \displaystyle \frac{a_3}{a_2} = </m><answer>-x/2</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m> \displaystyle \frac{a_4}{a_3} = </m><answer>-x/2</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        This series is <choiceInput inline name="S1">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>geometric</choice>
          <choice>not geometric</choice>
        </choiceInput>
        because the ratio between consecutive terms is <choiceInput inline name="S1a">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>always the same</choice>
          <choice>sometimes different</choice>
        </choiceInput>
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><award><when>
          $S1.selectedIndices=2 and $S1a.selectedIndices=2
        </when></award></answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m> \displaystyle S = 1 - x + \frac{x^2}{2} - \frac{x^3}{3} + \cdots + (-1)^n \frac{x^n}{n} + \cdots </m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m> \displaystyle \frac{a_3}{a_2} = </m><answer>-x/2</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m> \displaystyle \frac{a_4}{a_3} = </m><answer>-2x/3</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        This series is <choiceInput inline name="S2">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>geometric</choice>
          <choice>not geometric</choice>
        </choiceInput>
        because the ratio between consecutive terms is <choiceInput inline name="S2a">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>always the same</choice>
          <choice>sometimes different</choice>
        </choiceInput>
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><award><when>
          $S2.selectedIndices=3 and $S2a.selectedIndices=3
        </when></award></answer>
      </p>
    </li> 
    <li>
      <p>
        <m> \displaystyle S = 1 + x^2 + x^4 + x^6 + \cdots + x^{2n} + \cdots </m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m> \displaystyle \frac{a_3}{a_2} = </m><answer>x^2</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m> \displaystyle \frac{a_4}{a_3} = </m><answer>x^2</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        This series is <choiceInput inline name="S3">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>geometric</choice>
          <choice>not geometric</choice>
        </choiceInput>
        because the ratio between consecutive terms is <choiceInput inline name="S3a">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>always the same</choice>
          <choice>sometimes different</choice>
        </choiceInput>
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><award><when>
          $S3.selectedIndices=2 and $S3a.selectedIndices=2
        </when></award></answer>
      </p>
    </li>    
    <li>
      <p>
        <m> \displaystyle S = 1 + x + \frac{x^2}{2!} + \frac{x^3}{3!} + \cdots + \frac{x^n}{n!} + \cdots </m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m> \displaystyle \frac{a_3}{a_2} = </m><answer>x/2</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m> \displaystyle \frac{a_4}{a_3} = </m><answer>x/3</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        This series is <choiceInput inline name="S4">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>geometric</choice>
          <choice>not geometric</choice>
        </choiceInput>
        because the ratio between consecutive terms is <choiceInput inline name="S4a">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>always the same</choice>
          <choice>sometimes different</choice>
        </choiceInput>
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><award><when>
          $S4.selectedIndices=3 and $S4a.selectedIndices=3
        </when></award></answer>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>